<Post title="Why own the bus" part={1} date="2026-08-16">
	<p class="tagline">
		My ducted air conditioning is controlled by a cheap Android tablet on the wall. This
		series is about replacing everything between that tablet and the control box in the
		ceiling — before the vendor's hardware gets to choose for me.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the system</h2>
	<p>
		The house has a ducted reverse-cycle system: a Daikin unit doing the actual heating and
		cooling, and an Advantage Air "MyAir" control box in the ceiling that drives the zone
		dampers, reads the temperature sensors, and owns the control logic. The user-facing half
		of the system is a wall-mounted Android tablet running the vendor's MyPlace app, talking
		to the control box over a single cable that carries both power and data.
	</p>
	<p>
		It works well. The zoning is genuinely good. The problem isn't what the system does —
		it's who owns the ability to keep it doing that.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the problem</h2>
	<p>
		The wall tablet is the weakest link in the whole system, and it's also the part the
		vendor treats as a fuse: when it dies, the official answer is a new control system. The
		write-up at
		<a href="https://blog.hopefullyuseful.com/blog/advantage-air-ezone-tablet-diy-repair/"
			>blog.hopefullyuseful.com</a
		>
		tells the story well — OEM tablet suffers the classic eMMC failure six months out of
		warranty, support quotes <strong>$1,697</strong> for a replacement tablet, control box,
		and wireless sensors, because "new phones come out every year". The air conditioner was
		fine. The control box was fine. They were being asked to replace a five-figure system's
		control electronics because the cheapest component in the chain failed.
	</p>
	<p>
		That post has a happy ending (a 2015 Galaxy Tab 4 and some apktool surgery), and the
		Hacker News discussion around it kicked off
		<a href="https://github.com/gundy/aa_interop">aa_interop</a> — a community effort to
		document the hardware, protocol, and API. But the structural problem remains: if you run
		this system as shipped, your ability to turn your heater on is gated by a vendor's
		app-store listing, a vendor-signed app pair, and a tablet with a known mortality date.
	</p>
	<p>
		I didn't want to wait for my turn. The swap was <strong>proactive</strong>: the OEM
		tablet still worked when I started. The motivator was avoiding vendor lock-in in the
		future — owning the control path end-to-end while everything was healthy enough to
		experiment on safely.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>goals</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Zero functionality loss.</strong> The wall panel must look and behave exactly
			as it does stock — zones, schedules, temps, the lot. Non-negotiable; other people live
			here.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Instant rollback.</strong> Every stage must be reversible in minutes, with
			physical-layer rollback as the escape hatch at every step.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Local-first.</strong> No cloud dependency in the control path.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Reproducible.</strong> Every step documented well enough that someone else
			(or future-me, post-amnesia) can repeat it. This series is that documentation.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>constraints</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>One talker on the bus.</strong> The control box speaks half-duplex RS-485 and
			expects exactly one tablet-side endpoint. Two simultaneous talkers corrupt frames —
			so every migration step has to be a clean handover, never a shared bus.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>No vendor documentation.</strong> Everything known about the wire protocol
			comes from <a href="https://github.com/gundy/aa_interop">aa_interop</a>, which
			reverse-engineered a single CB9 control box. Verify everything against this install.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>It's production.</strong> This is the house's heating and cooling. Debugging
			sessions have a comfort budget.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>where we end up</h2>
	<Mermaid>{`flowchart TB
		subgraph before["Before"]
			direction LR
			CB1["Control Box"] <-->|"RS-485 + power"| OEM["OEM wall tablet<br/>(locked-down vendor Android)"]
		end
		subgraph after["After"]
			direction LR
			CB2["Control Box"] <-->|"RS-485"| PI["Pi Zero W<br/>cb-daemon (Rust)"]
			PI <-->|"WebSocket"| TAB["My tablet, my OS<br/>(vendor apps, repackaged)"]
		end
		before ==> after`}</Mermaid>

